UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 15, Lesson 1: Comparing two poems', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Comparing two poems means identifying what is similar and different in topic, language and feeling, using evidence from both texts.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCompare openings: \"Soft rain tapped the roof\" and \"Rain smashed the tin.\" Name one similarity and one difference.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSimilarity: both are about rain on a roof/tin. Difference: tapped feels gentle; smashed feels violent.\n\nCHECK\nListing every line from one poem without referring to the other is not a comparison.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRead two short poems on a related topic. Complete a chart: topic, mood, one language feature each, one similarity, one difference. Quote evidence for each point.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Comparing two poems means identifying what is similar and different in topic, language and feeling, using evidence from both texts.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01","EN2-UARL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Read two short poems on a related topic. Complete a chart: topic, mood, one language feature each, one similarity, one difference. Quote evidence for each point. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Poem A is about rain with soft s sounds; Poem B is about rain with crashing onomatopoeia. Both share rain; their sound choices create different moods.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Listing every line from one poem without referring to the other is not a comparison. Correction: Similarity: both are about rain on a roof/tin. Difference: tapped feels gentle; smashed feels violent.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Listing every line from one poem without referring to the other is not a comparison. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Comparing two poems means identifying what is similar and different in topic, language and feeling, using evidence from both texts. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Comparing two poems means identifying what is similar and different in topic, language and feeling, using evidence from both texts.","Listing every line from one poem without referring to the other is not a comparison.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Explain the evidence\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Listing every line from one poem without referring to the other is not a comparison.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to an independent example\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Similarity: both are about rain on a roof/tin. Difference: tapped feels gentle; smashed feels violent. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["Similarity: both are about rain on a roof/tin. Difference: tapped feels gentle; smashed feels violent.","Listing every line from one poem without referring to the other is not a comparison.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Compare openings: \"Soft rain tapped the roof\" and \"Rain smashed the tin.\" Name o\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["The most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Underline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Similarity: both are about rain on a roof/tin. Difference: tapped feels gentle; smashed feels violent. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["Similarity: both are about rain on a roof/tin. Difference: tapped feels gentle; smashed feels violent.","A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Listing every line from one poem without referring to the other is not a comparison.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a co\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material. Useful feedback uses the original evidence or language point.","options":["The new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After