pied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPoem A is about rain with soft s sounds; Poem B is about rain with crashing onomatopoeia. Both share rain; their sound choices create different moods.\n\nTRY FIRST\nCompare openings: \"Soft rain tapped the roof\" and \"Rain smashed the tin.\" Name one similarity and one difference.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSimilarity: both are about rain on a roof/tin. Difference: tapped feels gentle; smashed feels violent.","explain":"Comparing two poems means identifying what is similar and different in topic, language and feeling, using evidence from both texts.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Listing every line from one poem without referring to the other is not a comparison. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Two rain poems with different sound moods.","caption":"Compare both similarity and difference.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Shared topic</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">rain</text>